{
  "key": "solar_orbiter",
  "name": "Solar Orbiter",
  "rocket": "Atlas V 411",
  "payload": "Solar Orbiter (1,800 kg at launch)",
  "status": "En Route",
  "significance": "Solar Orbiter was built to do what no prior solar mission could: get close-up, high-resolution images of the Sun's surface AND look down on its polar regions from well outside the ecliptic plane, at the same time. Ulysses had studied the Sun's poles in situ decades earlier but carried no camera capable of imaging them; Parker Solar Probe flies far closer to the Sun but stays confined near the ecliptic. Solar Orbiter instead uses a long chain of Venus gravity assists to progressively tilt its orbit out of the ecliptic while also tightening its perihelion to under a third of an AU, combining remote-sensing imagers with in-situ instruments sampling the same solar wind it is photographing the source of. Its early images already resolved 'campfires' -- small-scale coronal brightenings across the Sun's surface -- and it has directly linked specific solar wind streams measured in situ back to the coronal holes they visibly originated from, a connection previous missions could only infer indirectly.",
  "statusNote": "ESA-led mission with NASA participation. Trajectory design uses repeated Venus gravity assists in resonant orbits to progressively raise orbital inclination out of the ecliptic (reaching 24 degrees by the end of the nominal mission, potentially 33 degrees in an extended mission) while also lowering perihelion toward roughly 0.29 AU -- a similar goal to Ulysses' single Jupiter flyby, achieved instead through many smaller repeated Venus encounters plus one early Earth flyby. Flybys after mid-2026 use ESA's published mission-design dates for the nominal and potential extended mission, not yet-observed telemetry. The 2026-12-24 Venus flyby was cross-checked on 2026-11-25 against JPL Horizons (target -144): closest approach 22:59 UTC at 945 km altitude.",
  "legs": [
    {
      "_comment": "Real Solar Orbiter trajectory on this leg dips to ~0.52 AU perihelion around 2020-06-10 then swings back out to ~0.99 AU around 2020-08-09 before heading to Venus -- a real >360deg (just over one full revolution) sweep in true anomaly, per JPL Horizons (target -144, checked 2026-08). A plain 2-point Lambert solve between the Feb and Dec endpoints misreads that mod-360 as an ~83deg short sweep, producing a wrong-direction orbit that balloons out to ~1.9 AU (past Mars) instead of diving toward the Sun. Split into three legs through two real recorded waypoints so each piece's sweep stays safely under 360deg and the solver can't misread it.",
